# NOTICE — DSH Cinema

DSH Cinema (skill name `dsh-cinema`) is an original work: every engine module, material routine, camera model, character
rig, audio synthesiser and QA rule in this repository was written for this project. No third-party source code is vendored
or copied into the tree.

## Runtime dependencies (not vendored)

| component | how it is used | licence |
|---|---|---|
| [puppeteer-core](https://github.com/puppeteer/puppeteer) | drives headless Chrome to rasterise frames; installed by `npm install` in a scaffolded project | Apache-2.0 |
| [Chrome / Chromium](https://www.chromium.org/) | the rasteriser itself; already installed on the machine, never redistributed | BSD-3-Clause and others |
| [FFmpeg](https://ffmpeg.org/) | encodes frames to H.264/AAC and muxes the mix; invoked as an external binary | LGPL-2.1-or-later / GPL depending on build; this project only *calls* it |

Because FFmpeg is invoked as a separate process rather than linked, its licence does not extend to this project. If you
bundle FFmpeg with your own distribution, follow its licence terms.

## Artistic references

The visual language is informed by documentary photography, minimalist and experimental cinema, and modern installation
art as *traditions*. No director's shot, no photographer's frame and no artwork is reproduced, traced or copied. The films
in `examples/` are generated entirely by the code in `template/` and are covered by the same MIT licence.

## Prior name

This project was developed as `documentary-code-cinema` and renamed to `dsh-cinema` at v1.0.0. The skill content is the
same work; the rename is recorded in `CHANGELOG.md` so existing users can follow it.
